The install worked. Only the final ansible --version check failed, and Molecule itself printed its version fine. The cause is a small pip package called selinux that got installed. It checks for system SELinux bindings, and RHEL has none for Python 3.11. Removing that one package fixes it, and I confirmed that in a clean copy of the same setup: molecule and ansible both ran afterward.

Run this as root on ansible-forge:

/opt/ansible/molecule/venv/bin/pip uninstall -y selinux && /opt/ansible/molecule/venv/bin/ansible --version | head -1

It should print ansible [core 2.15.13].

I’ll add that uninstall line to install-molecule.sh as well, in case you ever rebuild the virtualenv.

Then, as the ansible user, check Docker access. The group change only applies after a fresh login:

su - ansible -c 'source /opt/ansible/molecule/venv/bin/activate && molecule --version | head -1 && docker ps >/dev/null && echo docker-ok'

Send me the output. If it prints docker-ok, I’ll write the first Grafana 12 scenario around configure_grafana_stack. If you’d like me to start on that now, say so and I’ll begin with the scenario layout.



